One detail on mobile: a phone can't reach localhost on your computer. The app's settings take the computer's LAN IP instead (for example &lt;a href="http://192.168.1.20:8080/translator/api" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;http://192.168.1.20:8080/translator/api&lt;/a&gt;), and the Android emulator uses 10.0.2.2.
I also wrote a small Python mock of the API: same URLs, same JSON, same Basic auth. With it, I could build and test every client without the Java server or an API key.
What I'd do next
The result is one service, two interchangeable LLM backends, and four clients that share one contract. Gemini gives the more natural Darija; the local model is weaker but runs fully offline with no API key.
Next steps I'd take:
• Replace Basic auth with token-based auth (JWT), so clients don't store passwords.
• Stream the translation as it is generated, for long texts.
• Add the reverse direction, Darija to English or French.
Links
