&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Quick answer: Reuse an OpenAPI component with $ref whenever the same schema, parameter, or response is genuinely one concept used in multiple places; it gives you a single source of truth and keeps generated clients from sprouting duplicate types. Inline duplication is the right call for one-off structures or fields that look alike but are expected to change independently.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What components and $ref actually do
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The components object is a registry of reusable design elements: schemas, parameters, request bodies, responses, headers, examples, security schemes, links, and callbacks. Nothing in components is exposed on its own; it becomes part of an operation only when something points at it with a JSON reference such as &lt;code&gt;$ref: '#/components/schemas/Customer'&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A reference is a pointer, not a copy. Every operation that points to Customer resolves to the same definition, so the field set, types, and constraints stay identical by construction. The same mechanism works across files in a split project, where a relative path plus a pointer reaches into another document. This makes reuse mechanical rather than a matter of discipline, one of the structural habits the &lt;a href="https://www.powerduck.com/blog/openapi-guide-hub/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;OpenAPI guide&lt;/a&gt; returns to throughout.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why duplication drifts
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the same structure is pasted inline in several operations, each copy starts identical and then evolves on its own. A pagination parameter gains a different maximum on one endpoint; the standard error response picks up an extra field in one path but not others. Reviewing the document, the differences are hard to spot because they are separated by hundreds of lines.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Code generation makes the cost visible. A generator that sees a referenced component emits one model and reuses it; the same shape inlined four times yields four named types with identical fields, swelling the SDK and forcing consumers to convert between them. Lint rules flag unused components and structural duplicates, but cannot infer that two copies were meant to stay in sync.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Reuse vs duplication at a glance
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Situation&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Prefer&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Reason&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Same parameter or response on many operations&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Component plus $ref&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One edit updates every operation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One-off structure used in a single place&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Inline&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A named component adds noise&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Fields that look alike but mean different things&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Separate definitions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;They should not change together&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Common error envelope or pagination wrapper&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Shared component&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Stable concept that evolves as one&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Local variation of a shared model&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Component with a narrow override&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Keeps the common core, allows the difference&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  When shared schemas are the wrong call
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reuse has a real cost when two structures share a shape but not a meaning. A region string and a tier string may both be enumerated text today, but binding them to one component means a change to one forces a decision about the other. The test is whether the pieces represent the same concept and are expected to change together, not whether they look the same right now.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Watch the size of the shared surface too. A god component referenced everywhere turns a small local change into a broad regression, because every operation inherits it. If a component keeps accumulating optional fields to serve different callers, that is a signal to split it rather than extend it. One version note: in OpenAPI 3.0, sibling keywords next to $ref are ignored, while 3.1 allows them, so a ref meant to carry a local override has to match the version you use.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How to refactor without changing the contract
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pulling duplicated blocks into components should be a no-op for consumers. Identify the inline copies, extract one with a stable name, replace each copy with a $ref, then run a lint pass and compare the resolved output. Because the resolved document describes the same shapes, generated types should collapse rather than change meaning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After a refactor, confirm the contract rather than trusting the tidy file. In Powerduck you can import the updated spec and send requests against the operations you touched, checking that the same payloads still validate and return the expected responses, which catches a mistyped pointer or a broken cross-file reference before it ships.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Frequently asked questions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Does putting a schema in components expose it as an endpoint?&lt;/strong&gt; No. Components are definitions, not routes. A schema is reachable only where an operation references it, and the API surface does not change just because a component exists.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Should every inline schema be extracted?&lt;/strong&gt; No. A simple structure used once is clearer inline. Extract when the same concept appears in more than one place or is referenced by other components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Can I override a field next to a $ref?&lt;/strong&gt; In OpenAPI 3.1 sibling keywords are allowed, but in 3.0 they are ignored. For 3.0, model the variation with a composed schema instead of relying on siblings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How do I find duplicated definitions?&lt;/strong&gt; Lint tools such as Spectral, Redocly, and Vacuum flag unused components and compare structural shapes, so duplicates surface in CI rather than in a code review.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The bottom line
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Components exist to make define once, reference everywhere the default, and the payoff is a document that cannot quietly drift and a generated client without redundant types. Reuse the concepts that genuinely move together, keep one-off structures inline, and treat any refactor as a resolved-output comparison backed by real requests. Do that and shared components stay a tool for clarity rather than a source of coupling.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Disclaimer: I am not the person who predicts the market. I am a junior backend developer who reads job postings, gets rejected, and adjusts. So treat this as field notes, not a forecast.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But there is one thing I am sure of, because I lived it this year: &lt;strong&gt;the hard part of my job stopped being writing the code.&lt;/strong&gt; The hard part is reading what an agent wrote, at speed, and knowing whether it is right.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the skill worth building in 2027.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What actually changed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two years ago, the bottleneck was typing. You knew what you wanted; producing it took hours. Agents compressed that to minutes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The bottleneck moved. Now a model produces a plausible solution in forty seconds, and you have maybe three minutes to decide if it is correct, idiomatic, safe, and consistent with the rest of your codebase. If you cannot do that, the agent did not make you faster. It made you risky.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is why "learn to prompt" is bad advice. Prompting is a skill measured in days. &lt;strong&gt;Reviewing generated code is a skill measured in years&lt;/strong&gt;, and it is built on the same foundations everyone keeps telling you to skip: data structures, complexity, how a database actually executes a query, how TCP works, how a process fails at 3am.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An agent will write you a function that does a linear scan inside a loop and looks perfectly reasonable. If you do not know why that is O(n²), you ship it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The five things I would focus on
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Fundamentals, but for a different reason than before.&lt;/strong&gt; Not to write code from memory, but to audit code you did not write. That is a different exercise. Reading &lt;code&gt;EXPLAIN ANALYZE&lt;/code&gt; matters more than ever, because the agent will not run it for you with judgment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Linux and the terminal, properly.&lt;/strong&gt; Agents live in your shell. They run commands, read files, spawn processes. The developers who get the most out of them are the ones who already know what the command should have been. If you cannot debug a service that will not start, the agent's confident explanation will convince you of the wrong thing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. One agent, configured well.&lt;/strong&gt; Not five installed and half-used. The difference between a mediocre and a good agent setup is not the model. It is context: what it knows about your project, what it can read, what it is allowed to do, and whether it remembers yesterday.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. MCP, and how tools reach a model.&lt;/strong&gt; The Model Context Protocol is how an agent talks to your database, your browser, your docs. Learning to wire one tool properly teaches you more about agents than ten tutorials.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;5. Verification.&lt;/strong&gt; Tests, diffs, reproductions. This is the whole game. An agent that cannot prove its work is a guess with good formatting. This is also the thing that makes you employable, because it is the thing that is hard to fake.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I would stop doing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Collecting frameworks.&lt;/strong&gt; Another JS meta-framework in 2027 is a commodity. Nobody is hiring for it specifically.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Watching "AI for developers" content without building anything.&lt;/strong&gt; I did this for weeks. It is entertainment.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Learning tools that only work inside a vendor's IDE.&lt;/strong&gt; Prefer tools that run in your terminal and own your files.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The setup I actually use
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I run OpenCode v2, configured with Gentle-AI. Here is the reasoning, and then the exact install.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why OpenCode.&lt;/strong&gt; It is open source, it runs in the terminal, it is not tied to one model provider, and it is extensible through agents, skills, commands, plugins and MCP servers. If you are on Linux and live in a terminal, that combination is hard to beat. It also ships as a desktop app and a web interface if you want them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why Gentle-AI on top.&lt;/strong&gt; An agent with no workflow forgets everything between sessions, has no opinion about your project, and cannot prove what it did. Gentle-AI adds three things: persistent memory (Engram), a workflow for how work gets done (ODD), and an evidence-based review step (RDD). It also gives your agent a large skills library that loads on demand.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Important, and the project says it plainly: &lt;strong&gt;Gentle-AI never installs an AI agent for you.&lt;/strong&gt; It configures the one you already have. That is a good design, and it is worth knowing before you start.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Install OpenCode
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pick one method.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# curl (recommended on Linux)&lt;/span&gt;
curl -fsSL https://opencode.ai/v2/install &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; bash
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# Homebrew&lt;/span&gt;
brew install anomalyco/tap/opencode-v2
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# npm&lt;/span&gt;
npm install -g @opencode/cli
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# pnpm&lt;/span&gt;
pnpm add -g --allow-build=@opencode/cli @opencode/cli
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;On Arch there is also an AUR package, &lt;code&gt;paru -S opencode-beta&lt;/code&gt;, which tracks the beta channel.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;sudo pacman -S opencode
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Check it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;opencode --version
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The first run opens the terminal interface. Before you ask it anything, connect a model:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;/connect
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That opens the provider picker. You can bring your own API key, or use a subscription plan. Do not skip this and then wonder why the agent does nothing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Install Gentle-AI
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# macOS / Linux, script installer&lt;/span&gt;
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Gentleman-Programming/gentle-ai/main/scripts/install.sh &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; bash
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# Homebrew&lt;/span&gt;
brew install gentleman-programming/tap/gentle-ai
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# from source, needs Go&lt;/span&gt;
go install github.com/gentleman-programming/gentle-ai/v4/cmd/gentle-ai@latest
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If you use &lt;code&gt;go install&lt;/code&gt;, the binary lands in &lt;code&gt;$(go env GOPATH)/bin&lt;/code&gt;, which is not on your &lt;code&gt;PATH&lt;/code&gt; by default. Add it once, then open a new terminal:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;echo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"export PATH=\"$(go env GOPATH)/bin:\$PATH\""&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; ~/.bashrc
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then run the installer interface:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;gentle-ai
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fvrj724whu1m6ghls6hwy.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fvrj724whu1m6ghls6hwy.png" alt="Gentle-AI installer interface selecting agents and components" width="800" height="444"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It asks three things: which agents to configure, which components to install, and which persona to apply. You do not need Claude Code and Codex and Cursor at once. Start with OpenCode.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you prefer flags over the interface, or you want to preview first:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;gentle-ai install --agent opencode --dry-run
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;--dry-run&lt;/code&gt; prints the plan and touches nothing. Use it the first time. When you are happy with the plan, drop the flag.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then verify. &lt;code&gt;doctor&lt;/code&gt; is read-only; it never changes anything:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;gentle-ai doctor
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F7cnv73xoq3cznb08ddgm.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F7cnv73xoq3cznb08ddgm.webp" alt="Gentle-AI doctor output" width="351" height="327"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Back in OpenCode, the agent now has memory, a workflow, and a review step. It also keeps a snapshot of your config before every write it makes, which matters the first time you break something.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Updating both
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two independent things to keep current.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;OpenCode updates itself:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;opencode upgrade
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It detects how you installed it. If you installed with a package manager that owns the version, pass the method explicitly, for example &lt;code&gt;opencode upgrade --method brew&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Gentle-AI depends on how you installed it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# Homebrew&lt;/span&gt;
brew upgrade gentle-ai
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# script installer — re-run it&lt;/span&gt;
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Gentleman-Programming/gentle-ai/main/scripts/install.sh &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; bash
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# from source&lt;/span&gt;
go install github.com/gentleman-programming/gentle-ai/v4/cmd/gentle-ai@latest
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Updating the binary is not the last step. Managed configs and skills have to match the new version:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;gentle-ai sync
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;sync&lt;/code&gt; rewrites the managed files, keeps your own settings, and leaves a backup. Then run &lt;code&gt;gentle-ai doctor&lt;/code&gt; again. If you update only the binary and skip &lt;code&gt;sync&lt;/code&gt;, you end up with a new CLI reading old prompts, which is the kind of problem that looks like a model bug and is not.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;gentle-ai --version
opencode --version
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  What a session looks like
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;cd&lt;/span&gt; ~/projects/your-app
opencode
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fnuensay8ogs3au05mllk.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fnuensay8ogs3au05mllk.webp" alt="OpenCode terminal session working on a repository" width="800" height="473"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The difference from a chat window is that the agent reads your repository, runs your commands, and edits files. Which is exactly why the next part matters.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The part nobody puts in the announcement
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An agent with write access and no verification is a very fast way to introduce bugs you will not notice for a month. The first week I used one, I accepted a refactor that passed no tests, because there were no tests to pass.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So the workflow I would learn in 2027, in one line: &lt;strong&gt;make the agent prove it.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If the change is testable, write the test first and watch it fail. An agent that never sees a red test will happily ship a green one that tests nothing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Read the diff. All of it. Not the summary.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run the command yourself on the edge case the agent did not consider.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If it says "this should work", that is not evidence.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is not a limitation of the tools. It is the job now.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A 90-day plan, if you want one
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Days 1 to 15.&lt;/strong&gt; Install OpenCode and Gentle-AI. Use them on one real project, not a tutorial. Break something and fix it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Days 16 to 45.&lt;/strong&gt; Pick one thing you already know well and make the agent do it, then review the result line by line. You will find something. That moment is the skill.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Days 46 to 75.&lt;/strong&gt; Add one MCP server that connects the agent to something you actually use. Read how it works, not just how to install it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Days 76 to 90.&lt;/strong&gt; Write the setup down. A short post, a README, a dotfiles repo. Explaining your workflow is how you find out which parts of it are real.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Conclusion
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In 2027, "I know React" is not a differentiator. "I can direct an agent to build something and prove it works" is, because it requires everything underneath: fundamentals, Linux, testing, judgment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The tools will change. This post will be outdated before the year ends, and that is fine. The workflow it describes, memory, small steps, evidence, is the part that survives.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you take one thing from this article, take the sentence from the Gentle-AI README that I keep coming back to: &lt;strong&gt;verifying beats generating.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  TL;DR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A developer just ran Kimi K3, a 2.8-trillion-parameter model, on an ordinary CPU with 8 GB of RAM. The project is called kimi-k3-in-c: 176 KB of code, no GPU, no framework. The principle: the model never sits in memory as a whole, it lives on disk and only loads the fraction it needs for each word it generates. You can reproduce the experiment at home, provided you have 1.7 TB of NVMe SSD and accept 10 to 32 seconds per generated word.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  The wall everyone hits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The strongest AI models weigh hundreds of billions, sometimes trillions of parameters. Kimi K3, released by the Chinese lab Moonshot AI in late July 2026, holds 2.8 trillion. Getting answers comparable to GPT or Claude used to require a cluster of professional GPUs, out of reach for an individual or a small outfit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reason is mechanical: a model that size far exceeds the RAM of a consumer machine. The labs that train and serve it run on machines with several hundred gigabytes of VRAM, spread across multiple cards.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What one developer just demonstrated
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fareed Khan published &lt;a href="https://github.com/FareedKhan-dev/kimi-k3-in-c" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;kimi-k3-in-c&lt;/a&gt;, an inference engine for Kimi K3 written in pure C99. No PyTorch, no CUDA, no external math library. The repository holds up to inspection: Apache 2.0 license, active continuous integration, and a test suite that compares every computation against a PyTorch reference.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On the most modest machine tested, with 8 GB of RAM, the engine generates coherent text:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight console"&gt;&lt;code&gt;$ ./bin/k3 ~/k3model --preset laptop --prompt &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"The capital of France is"&lt;/span&gt; --gen 8
--- generated text ---
 Paris.&lt;span class="s2"&gt;", "&lt;/span&gt;The Eiffel
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The model answers correctly. It runs on a CPU. It fits in a laptop's RAM.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The principle, in one sentence
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The model file weighs 1.56 TB on disk: no consumer RAM can hold it whole. kimi-k3-in-c works around the problem by keeping in memory only what it needs right now, and fetching the rest from disk at the precise moment it needs it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Kimi K3 is a Mixture-of-Experts model: out of 896 internal experts, only 16 fire to produce a given word. kimi-k3-in-c exploits that property. It keeps a small part of the model resident in memory, and loads only the 16 required experts from disk for each word, rather than all 896 at once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What it changes in practice
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can reproduce this on hardware you may already own: an AVX2-capable CPU, 8 GB of RAM, and above all 1.7 TB of NVMe SSD, the real prerequisite behind this project. Downloading the model alone takes several hours.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This project does not prove that a CPU replaces a GPU. It proves that a model designed for hundreds of gigabytes of VRAM can, with the right software architecture, run correctly on resources a thousand times smaller. The barrier is no longer only a matter of budget: part of it has become a matter of engineering.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One question remains, and we will dig into it separately: how fast. The answer tempers the enthusiasm of the headline considerably. &lt;a href="https://dimitriaigle.fr/articles/comment-fonctionne-kimi-k3-in-c-en" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;How kimi-k3-in-c runs 2.8 trillion parameters on 8 GB of RAM&lt;/a&gt; explains the mechanism in detail, and &lt;a href="https://dimitriaigle.fr/articles/rien-ne-remplacera-les-gpu-en" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Why a GPU is still essential&lt;/a&gt; explains why this is not usable day to day yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Short answer: keep avatar metadata and retention state in a queryable database, and treat object storage listing by prefix as a reconciliation input rather than a search engine. For a B2B SaaS product-image service that creates tenant-scoped exports, this split makes deletion provable even when the bucket can only list keys under a prefix.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The constraint is easy to miss. A key such as &lt;code&gt;tenants/acme/avatars/u_42/original.webp&lt;/code&gt; tells you where bytes live; it does not reliably answer “which objects are &lt;code&gt;image/webp&lt;/code&gt; and tagged &lt;code&gt;profile&lt;/code&gt;?” Content type and tags belong in the metadata record you control. The object remains the payload.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What should a tenant avatar export query before object listing?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with the business query. An export request might ask for active avatars uploaded by tenant &lt;code&gt;acme&lt;/code&gt; during a date window, restricted to &lt;code&gt;image/webp&lt;/code&gt;, and labeled &lt;code&gt;profile&lt;/code&gt;. SQL can filter those fields, enforce tenant ownership, and return a stable set of object keys. Only then should a worker fetch bytes or issue short-lived download URLs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is tempting to treat a prefix as if it were an index. An export can look fine, then include a deleted avatar because a listing was cached for a few minutes. That is a data-lifecycle mistake, not a storage outage. The repair is a metadata row with &lt;code&gt;deleted_at&lt;/code&gt;, an object generation, and an export snapshot ID.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use a deliberately boring record:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Field&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Purpose&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Deletion implication&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;tenant_id, avatar_id&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Isolation and identity&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Every delete is scoped and auditable&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;object_key, generation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Exact payload reference&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Prevents deleting a newer replacement&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;content_type, tags&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Search and policy filters&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No full bucket scan for metadata&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;created_at, deleted_at&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Retention clock&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Makes eligibility deterministic&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;export_id, state&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Snapshot membership&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A running export can be cancelled safely&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The object key can still be prefix-friendly. Put tenant and lifecycle state in the key for operational scans, but do not pretend that key order encodes every predicate. Tags are useful at the object boundary for policy checks; they are a poor substitute for a tenant-aware query index when a request spans many tags or time ranges.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep bytes dumb.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How can content type, tags, and prefix limits shape deletion evidence?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Deletion needs two independent facts: the database says an object is eligible, and storage confirms the exact version is gone. Record both events under one request ID. A successful &lt;code&gt;DELETE&lt;/code&gt; response is an HTTP result, not proof that a later list will immediately be empty; cache behavior and listing consistency rules still deserve a test against your chosen store.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the control flow I use in a worker. It never deletes by a broad prefix, and it refuses to act when the generation it read is no longer current.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dataclasses&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dataclass&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;datetime&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;datetime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;timezone&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@dataclass&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;frozen&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;True&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;AvatarObject&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;tenant_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;avatar_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;object_key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;generation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;deleted_at&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;datetime&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;None&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;deletion_candidates&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;list&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;AvatarObject&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;datetime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;list&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;AvatarObject&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;deleted_at&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;is&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;not&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;None&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;and&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;deleted_at&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;delete_one&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;store&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;AvatarObject&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;None&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# The storage adapter must use an exact-generation precondition.&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;store&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;delete&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;object_key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;generation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;generation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;request_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;request_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The adapter should emit &lt;code&gt;delete_started&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;delete_confirmed&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;delete_rejected&lt;/code&gt; events with the tenant, object key hash, generation, policy version, and retention deadline. Do not log the image URL or user-supplied tag values verbatim; those strings often contain emails or internal ticket numbers. Keep the audit event longer than the bytes, but document that split in the retention policy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A prefix listing still has a job. Run it as a reconciler that finds unreferenced keys, stale multipart remnants, or objects whose metadata row was never committed. In a dry run, the useful output is a manifest of keys with no matching row; some belong to uploads whose database transaction has not committed yet, some are test fixtures, and the rest need an owner. That is why reconciliation should quarantine a mismatch first, attach the first-seen timestamp and source prefix, and require an explicit policy decision. Deleting every key not seen in SQL is dangerous during an in-flight upload or a replica delay, and a retrying worker can make the same orphan appear in several scans unless the reconciler stores its own observation ID.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Which storage boundaries are unsuitable for avatar search and exports?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prefix-only listing is unsuitable as the primary metadata search when customers can define arbitrary tags, when exports must be repeatable, or when deletion has legal or contractual deadlines. Choose a database-backed index and a queue in those cases. Keep a direct listing workflow for small maintenance jobs where the prefix is the actual policy boundary and the operator can tolerate an eventually reconciled result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The catch is operational ownership. A richer metadata index adds migrations, backfills, and privacy review. A storage-only design has fewer tables but pushes query semantics into brittle scans and makes “deleted” hard to prove. Your mileage may vary with very large tenants; partition metadata by tenant and time, and rate-limit export workers so one account cannot monopolize listing or download bandwidth.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The comparison is about boundaries, not brand names:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Design&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Good fit&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Limitation to state plainly&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;SQL metadata plus object payloads&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Tag search, repeatable exports, deletion audits&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Requires schema ownership and backfill jobs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Object tags plus prefix listing&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Simple policy checks and small buckets&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cross-tag queries and history are awkward&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Search index plus object storage&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Fast faceted discovery at scale&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Index lag must be reconciled before deletion&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do not use object count as the success metric. Measure export snapshot drift, deletion lag, orphan count, and the percentage of deletes with a matching generation. A five-minute dashboard gap can be acceptable; an unbounded orphan queue is not.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A rollout that makes retention testable
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;First, dual-write metadata for new uploads while the existing path remains authoritative. Backfill in tenant-sized batches, recording the source prefix and a checksum for every discovered key. Then run exports in shadow mode: produce a manifest and compare it with the current result without sending it to a customer. I would fail the rollout on any cross-tenant key, even if the total count matches.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Next, enable deletion for one tenant with a short test retention window. Kill a worker after the database transaction, after the storage delete, and before the audit event. The retry must converge on the same generation and request ID; a replacement avatar must survive because its generation differs. This is the edge case that usually exposes an over-broad &lt;code&gt;delete(prefix)&lt;/code&gt; helper.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Finally, publish the contract to support and compliance: what “deleted” means, how long audit metadata remains, and how a legal hold pauses eligibility. Keep the API surface small. The policy is the product; the bucket is an implementation detail.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.backblaze.com/cloud-storage/pricing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.backblaze.com/cloud-storage/pricing&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/ListingKeysUsingAPIs.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/ListingKeysUsingAPIs.html&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/listing-objects" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/listing-objects&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/storageservices/list-blobs2" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/storageservices/list-blobs2&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Draw and Guess scores a guess from the time left and the order of the guess. The rule is short, and it makes a good small example of designing a score you can explain. This is how it reads as code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The speed points
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The constants are a floor of 100 and a range of 400. The speed points for a guess are the floor plus the range times the fraction of time left, rounded to a multiple of five:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;speed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;round5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;100&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;400&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;left&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;At the default 100 seconds an instant guess scores 500 and a last-second one 100. Each second is worth four points. On a 40-second drawing every second is worth ten points.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The order share
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first right guess is paid every speed point. The second keeps about 56%, the third about 38%, the fourth about 29%, the fifth about 24%, and no later guess falls below 20%. A formula that fits those numbers is:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;share(k) = max(0.2, 1 / (1 + 0.8 * (k - 1)))
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I fitted that to the published shares. It is a description, not the source.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The drawer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The drawer gets the mean of everyone else's speed points, with a non-guesser counted as zero. Three guessers on 400, 325 and 250 give the drawer 325. If nobody guesses, the drawer scores nothing. In a room of two, the drawer gets what the one guess earned.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why this design
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Speed matters, but not too much.&lt;/strong&gt; The floor of 100 means a late right guess still scores.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Order matters, with a floor.&lt;/strong&gt; The share never falls below 20%, so the last right guesser is not ignored.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The drawer is tied to the group.&lt;/strong&gt; A clear drawing pays everyone.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A test
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Check your own score by hand for a three-player round. If your numbers do not match the screen, one of us has a bug.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Where to see it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can watch the formula work on &lt;a href="https://playboardit.com/play/draw-and-guess" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Boardit's Draw and Guess&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  When the hints land
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The score and the hints share a clock. On a 100-second drawing with three hints, the hints fall at 75, 50 and 25 seconds left, which are also the moments when a guess is worth 400, 300 and 200 points, so each hint arrives a quarter of the range lower.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The score in a line
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Floor plus range times time left, then a share by order.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Ahmed Moaz builds Boardit, a free browser game site.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;In one paragraph.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;18 U.S.C. § 2713 — the CLOUD Act — obliges a provider to "preserve, comply with, and produce" any data in its possession, custody or control, regardless of whether that data physically sits in the EU or abroad. GitHub is a wholly-owned subsidiary of Microsoft Corporation: a US company in a US jurisdiction. That makes every repository on github.com reachable by US process — even one whose files are served from Frankfurt. Codeberg, at the other end, is operated by Codeberg e.V., a registered non-profit association under German law, headquartered in Berlin. It is not a provider in the jurisdiction of the United States, so the Act never reaches it. For us — nine repositories on &lt;a href="https://codeberg.org/PrivateLocalAI" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;codeberg.org/PrivateLocalAI&lt;/a&gt;, an inference stack that runs in our own engine room — that is not ideology, it is a compliance decision with a file location.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A repository is personal data, full stop
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;People treat source code as if the GDPR did not apply to it. It does. A repository carries contributor names and email addresses (git author metadata is baked into every commit), branch and issue discussions, access logs, membership rosters. Under Article 4(1) of the GDPR most of that is personal data about identifiable people — maintainers, contributors, candidates — and once a US provider holds it, Chapter V of the GDPR says a third-country transfer has taken place. After &lt;em&gt;Schrems II&lt;/em&gt; (C-311/18), the organisation using the service carries the homework: standard contractual clauses alone are not enough; you need a transfer impact assessment and, where needed, supplementary measures that actually deliver essentially equivalent protection. We wrote the mechanics of this in an earlier post, &lt;a href="https://blog.localai.hex7.com/your-data-your-jurisdiction-how-local-ai-keeps-customer-data-out-of-the-us-cloud-acts-reach/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;"Your data, your jurisdiction"&lt;/a&gt; — nothing changes because the data happens to be C++ and Rust.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What the CLOUD Act actually compels
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The statute is unusually plain, which is why it bites. § 2713(a) requires a provider service to "preserve, comply with, and produce any communication, call, or other recording or other information in its possession, custody, or control regardless of the location of such communication, call, record or information." The old argument that US process only reaches data stored on US servers was specifically cancelled by the 2018 Act — &lt;em&gt;Microsoft Corp. v. Microsoft&lt;/em&gt;, the case the Act partly codifies, had died on exactly that question: a provider in Washington could be compelled to produce email stored in Ireland.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The test is not "where does the file sit". The test is whether the provider is an entity "in the jurisdiction of the United States." Microsoft — and therefore GitHub — plainly is. An EU-mirrored git host does not change that: Azure-hosted enterprise endpoints, Frankfurt regions, a sub-page served from Europe — all cosmetic against § 2713. The obligation attaches to the company, not the data centre.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why Codeberg e.V. is different in kind, not in promise
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Codeberg is not a company that found a friendlier cloud; it is an association — an &lt;em&gt;eingetragener Verein&lt;/em&gt; registered under German law, based in Berlin, non-profit by construction, run democratically by its members. The forge itself (codeberg.org) runs on Forgejo software, maintained by the community and hosted for the community by the association. The difference between Codeberg and GitHub is therefore not a difference of promises — it is a question of whose law governs the provider at all:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For GitHub: US jurisdiction first. US process reaches our repositories in Berlin, Tokyo or San Francisco; then GDPR becomes our headache to paper over with SCCs and assessments.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For Codeberg e.V.: German law and the GDPR apply directly, as domestic processing. Chapter V never even opens, because there is no third-country transfer to assess. No § 2713 obligation exists on the provider side for anyone to invoke.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is what "moving away from US-hosted services" actually accomplishes, stated honestly: not that American law disappears, but that it stops having any route into your systems at all. A company that keeps its inference on its own hardware and now keeps its source history with a Berlin non-profit has simply removed the transatlantic conflict from its compliance register — there is nothing left to assess, no SCC module to pick, no TIA file to keep updated, because the provider never falls within reach of US compelled disclosure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The honest caveats
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two, so this stays a serious post and not marketing:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Jurisdiction follows the provider, but so does pragmatism.&lt;/strong&gt; We still mirror everything; git is a distributed tool by design, and forks are free as in forkable. Moving &lt;em&gt;to&lt;/em&gt; Codeberg does not mean locking anything away — codeberg.org/PrivateLocalAI stays open, and a mirror is one &lt;code&gt;git push&lt;/code&gt; away from anywhere it used to be.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One forge does not make the stack compliant.&lt;/strong&gt; Our inference engines — llama.cpp, Strata, Avalon, FreeToken, Xolotl — run on hardware in our own building for exactly the same reason the repositories moved: an air-gapped or on-prem deployment never leaves the jurisdiction either. The git host change is one line in a register; the inference stack being physically ours is the big one. Both live or die by the same principle: &lt;em&gt;whatever you cannot transfer out of the jurisdiction cannot be compelled into it.&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your compliance team keeps asking whether your US AI or git vendor "tramples" European privacy law — and after Schrems II they must ask — there is a quieter answer than an assessment file. It lives in Frankfurt, Berlin, or your own rack. We put ours on the internet as codeberg.org/PrivateLocalAI.&lt;/p&gt;

